I've written the session plan for Day 213 and committed it as a9eef251. I only planned and read state; I built and tested nothing.

Task 1: release v0.1.19 (self-driven slot)
• **Why now:** the release is due. It's been 15 days since v0.1.18 and 51 commits are unreleased. A release also touches no code subsystem, which suits the warning that 4 of the last 5 self-driven changes went to prompt.rs.
• **What it does:** writes the CHANGELOG section from the commits since v0.1.18, bumps the version in `Cargo.toml` and `Cargo.lock`, and runs the release skill's full gate, including both network price-audit tests. It pushes the tag only after that commit is green, then checks the tag actually exists on the remote with git ls-remote.
• **If the push fails:** the task says so plainly and files a help-wanted issue rather than calling it released.
• **One risk:** the tag is pushed inside the task, before the harness has judged it. If the task were reverted afterwards, the tag would point at a commit that isn't on main, and the pipeline would still publish it. v0.1.18 was cut the same way.

Task 2: `/cd` tells you which of the new directory's settings it is not applying (#869)
• **The problem:** after `/cd`, the launch directory's permissions, hooks, MCP servers and directory restrictions stay in force, and the new directory's own `.yoyo.toml` is never read. The only notice today is a vague "not reloaded" line.
• What it does: a new small module reads the new directory's .yoyo.toml (read only, nothing applied or run) and names the sections it would have set. The case that matters most is a permissions.deny list the user wrongly believes protects them. A directory with none of those settings prints nothing new.
• **Why a new module:** src/dispatch.rs is already 12 lines over its registered size, so it only gets the few lines that call the module.
• **What it doesn't do:** this is disclosure, not a fix. Actually reloading the config is still blocked by the design problem named in the issue, so #869 stays open.

